window.PHASE_DATA_LN=window.PHASE_DATA_LN||{};window.PHASE_DATA_LN[7]={"pages":[{"pdf":163,"printed":148,"ch":7,"text":"CHAPTER 7\n\nLayer 6: Climate Finance, Central Banks\nand Climate Stress Testing\nBefore you start\nWhat Layer 6 is for. Layers 1 to 5 gave you the economics, regulation, contagion and asset-pricing tools. Layer 6 is where those tools meet climate: five readings that define climaterelated financial risk, show how central banks think about it, and review the evidence. Together they supply your thesis's definitions, its policy motivation, and its literature gap.\n\nSix concepts to hold in your head first\n• Physical risk vs transition risk. Physical risk is damage from climate events (floods, storms, sea-level rise). Transition risk is loss from the move to a low-carbon economy (carbon tax, technology shifts, changing preferences). Your thesis is about the second. Zhou et al. cover only the first.\n• Tragedy of the horizon. Carney's phrase for the fact that the worst climate costs fall beyond the time horizons of businesses, politicians and central banks, so nobody has a direct incentive to act.\n• Green swan. Bolton et al.'s term for a climate event that, unlike a black swan, is foreseeable in kind but deeply uncertain in timing and form, so it cannot be priced from history. Related ideas: stranded assets (reserves or plants that lose value) and the climate Minsky moment (a sudden collapse of asset prices).\n• Orderly vs disorderly transition. Orderly means policy comes early and predictably, so markets adjust smoothly. Disorderly means late and sudden, which risks fire sales and pro-cyclical losses.\n• Climate beta / climate risk premium. How an asset's return co-moves with climate shocks, from the stochastic discount factor you met in Layer 5. Giglio et al. define the climate beta as the covariance of consumption and the marginal social cost of emissions (p. 23, fn 2). Whether the premium is positive or negative depends on what kind of shock you model (pp. 19 to 20).\n• Climate stress test. A severe-but-plausible scenario applied to a balance sheet to estimate losses on profitability, solvency and liquidity (Zhou et al., p. 239). Monasterolo adds network amplification (pp. 313 to 315).\n\nMap of the five readings"},{"pdf":164,"printed":149,"ch":7,"text":"Table 7.1. Before you start: Map of the five readings\nReading\n\nWhat it is\n\nWhat it gives you\n\nLength to read\n\nCarney (2015)\n\nCentralbanker speech\n\nThe founding framing: horizon problem,\nthree risk types, disclosure and stress\ntesting\n\n16 pages, easy\n\nBolton et al.\n(2020)\n\nBIS/Banque\nde France\nbook\n\nGreen swan concept, why VaR fails,\nscenario analysis, Basel pillars\n\n115 pages; read the\nsummary and Chapters\n3 and 4 first\n\nGiglio, Kelly\nand Stroebel\n(2021)\n\nAsset-pricing\nreview\n\nTheory of the climate risk premium;\nevidence in equities, bonds, housing\n\n17 pages, moderate\n(some algebra)\n\nMonasterolo\n(2020)\n\nClimateTransmission channels to banks, climate\nfinance review VaR and spread, network stress test\n\n18 pages, moderate\n\nZhou,\nEndendijk and\nBotzen (2023)\n\nPhysical-risk\nreview\n\n18 pages, easy to\nmoderate\n\nBank, insurer and market effects of\ndisasters; econometric toolkit\n\nSuggested reading order\n1. Carney first. Short, and every other paper cites it.\n2. Giglio, Kelly and Stroebel. It reuses Layer 5 vocabulary and sets out the theory behind the others.\n3. Monasterolo. The closest to your capital-adequacy question.\n4. Zhou et al. The physical-risk and methods companion.\n5. Bolton et al. last. Longest, and easiest once you know the vocabulary. Start with the executive summary, then Chapters 3 and 4.\n\nHow to use these notes\nThe sections below follow this order: Bolton et al., Carney, Giglio et al., Monasterolo, Zhou et al. (the reading order above is the order I suggest you read the originals, not the order of the notes). Each section ends with thesis uses and file flags. Section 6 collects twelve quotables with page numbers, the four debates you can stage, and a record of what was and was not read. Page numbers are each source's own printed pages, not the PDF viewer's count.\n\n1. Bolton et al. (2020), The Green Swan: reading the file and writing\nthe notes\nWhat was read. Patrick Bolton, Morgan Despres, Luiz Awazu Pereira da Silva, Frédéric Samama and Romain Svartzman, The green swan: central banking and financial stability in the age of climate change, BIS and Banque de France, January 2020 (115 PDF pages; the book itself runs to p. 67 plus annexes). Page numbers below are the book's printed pages. I read the executive summary (pp. 1–4), the introduction (pp. 5–10), most of Chapter 2 (pp. 11–22, skipping 2.2 on distributional effects), Chapter 3 (pp. 23–43 except the middle of 3.1 on tipping points and Box 3), Chapter 4.1, 4.2 and 4.4 (pp. 50–54, 59–60) and the conclusion"},{"pdf":165,"printed":150,"ch":7,"text":"(pp. 65–67). Not read: the forewords, Chapters 4.3 (green fiscal–monetary coordination) and\n4.5 (accounting), and Annexes 1–4.\n\n1.1 The argument in six sentences\n1. Climate change is a source of financial and price instability, through physical risks (damage from climate hazards) and transition risks (disorderly mitigation) (p. 1).\n2. Climate risk has features that break standard risk management: deep uncertainty, nonlinearity, fat tails, and no history to extrapolate (p. 21).\n3. Scenario analysis and climate stress tests are the right response, but they are a partial one (p. 23).\n4. Climate risks stay largely unhedgeable for the system as a whole unless there is systemwide action (p. 24).\n5. So central banks should do their own job (supervision, stress tests) and also coordinate and call for broader action: the ”five Cs” (p. 2).\n6. If nothing is done, central banks may be forced to act as ”climate rescuers of last resort” (p. 1). Quotable (p. 1): ”climate-related risks will remain largely unhedgeable as long as system-wide action is not undertaken.”\n\n1.2 The green swan concept (Box A, p. 3)\nTaleb's black swan has three marks: unexpected and rare, extreme impact, explainable only after the fact. Such events fit fat-tailed distributions, so models that assume normality (valueat-risk) cannot predict them. The authors say climate risks share these features and differ in three ways:\n1. Near-certainty of materialisation. Their impacts are uncertain, but ”there is a high degree of certainty that some combination of physical and transition risks will materialize” (quoting NGFS 2019, p. 3).\n2. Higher stakes. Climate catastrophes could be more serious than most systemic financial crises, even existential.\n3. Higher complexity. Chain reactions and cascades across environmental, geopolitical, social and economic dynamics. They define the green swan as ”a new type of systemic risk that involves interacting, nonlinear, fundamentally unpredictable, environmental, social, economic and geopolitical dynamics” (p. 6). Quotable (p. 6): a green swan ”is a new type of systemic risk that involves interacting, nonlinear, fundamentally unpredictable, environmental, social, economic and geopolitical dynamics, which are irreversibly transformed by the growing concentration of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.”\n\n1.3 Why a carbon price alone is not enough (pp. 6–8)\nThe standard textbook answer (Pigou): set a carbon tax equal to the social cost of carbon and the market does the rest, so central banks have little to do (p. 6). The authors give three"},{"pdf":166,"printed":151,"ch":7,"text":"objections:\n• In practice, carbon prices are far too low to move capital from brown to green assets, and physical damage will come anyway, with consequences for financial stability and for prices through supply shocks (pp. 6–7).\n• Climate is ”the greatest market failure the world has ever seen” (Stern), so prices may need to jump sharply, which brings transition risk. Newly enforced regulation could cause ”a climate Minsky moment” (Carney 2018) (p. 7), a severe tightening of financial conditions for carbon-intensive firms. Carney's phrase: ”the paradox is that success is failure” (p. 7): the policies best for climate may be worst for short-run financial stability.\n• Socio-technical inertia. Higher prices alone do not change behaviour if the infrastructure (for example public transit) is not there (p. 7). Link to Layer 2: this is the policy-level echo of the Stern, Pindyck and Weitzman debate on carbon prices and discount rates.\n\n1.4 Physical and transition risks and how they reach banks (Chapter 2, pp. 17–20) Definitions (pp. 17–18).\n• Physical risks are ”those risks that arise from the interaction of climate-related hazards … with the vulnerability of exposure to human and natural systems” (quoting Batten et al. 2016): storms, floods, heat waves, sea-level rise, changes in rainfall. Non-insured losses are about 70% of weather-related losses (p. 17).\n• Transition risks come from a rapid move to a low-carbon economy: policy change, reputational impacts, technology, shifts in preferences. The headline example is stranded assets: a large part of proven fossil reserves cannot be burned (Box 1, p. 19: to have a 50% chance of staying under 2°C, over 80% of current coal reserves, half of gas and a third of oil would stay unused between 2010 and 2050, per McGlade and Ekins 2015).\n• A liability risk (lawsuits for climate damage) is mentioned in a footnote and usually folded into the other two (p. 17). Interaction (pp. 18–19). Strong early action raises transition risk and cuts physical risk. Delayed and weak action means higher physical risk with transition risk not removed. Delayed action followed by a catch-up could mean both are high. Five financial risk channels (pp. 19–20). Table 7.2. Bolton et al. (2020): 1.4 Physical and transition risks and how they reach banks (Chapter 2, pp. 17–20) Channel\n\nHow climate risk enters\n\nCredit risk\n\nBorrowers' ability to repay deteriorates (higher PD and LGD), collateral values fall (p. 19)\n\nMarket risk\n\nReassessment of profitability, fire sales, climate value-at-risk (p. 20)\n\nLiquidity risk\n\nBanks hit by credit and market losses may be unable to refinance, strain in\ninterbank markets (p. 20)"},{"pdf":167,"printed":152,"ch":7,"text":"Channel\n\nHow climate risk enters\n\nOperational risk\n\nOffices or data centres hit by physical events (p. 20)\n\nInsurance risk\n\nHigher claims, mispriced cover for new green technologies (p. 20)\n\nLink to Layer 4: the fire-sale and refinancing channels are the Brunnermeier–Pedersen loss and margin spirals, and the cascade of stranding across sectors (Cahen-Fourot et al., p. 39) is a network-contagion story like Acemoglu et al.\n\n1.5 Why standard risk management fails (Section 2.5, pp. 20–22)\n• VaR gives losses expected at 95–99% confidence over a short horizon; capital requirements are calculated on a one-year horizon from PD, EAD and LGD estimated from historical track records (p. 21).\n• ”extrapolating historical trends can only lead to mispricing of climate-related risks, as these risks have barely started to materialise”; they also ”concentrate precisely in the 1% not considered by VaR” (p. 21). Quotable (p. 21): ”green swan events cannot be captured by traditional risk management.”\n\n• The authors borrow Bachelard's idea of an epistemological break (and Kuhn's\nparadigm shift): intellectual habits that were useful become an obstacle. The break is already happening in practice, through forward-looking, scenario-based methods (pp. 21–22). For a capital-adequacy thesis, this is the central argument: if PD, LGD and the one-year capital horizon rest on history, they will understate climate risk.\n\n1.6 Scenario analysis and its limits (Chapter 3, pp. 23–43)\nKey conclusion (p. 23): forward-looking analyses ”cannot fully overcome the limitations of the probabilistic approaches … and provide sufficient hedging against 'green swan' events.” There are two limitations: (1) the interactions are nonlinear and deeply uncertain, so models cannot represent them; (2) risk stays unhedgeable without system-wide action (p. 24). Three methodological steps and their problems:\n1. Choosing the scenario (3.1–3.2, pp. 25–36). A scenario fixes the climate target (2°C or 1.5°C), the timing of mitigation (immediate vs delayed and abrupt) and the type of shock (carbon tax or technology). Most use integrated assessment models (IAMs) with a climate module, an economic module and a damage function (p. 26). Known weaknesses: damage functions, discount rates, no endogenous structural change, unrealistic generalequilibrium assumptions, smooth transitions (p. 26). The authors cite Pindyck's remark that the social cost of carbon and the discount rate can give ”almost any result one desires” (p. 27). Technological assumptions matter hugely: scenarios with large carbon capture and negative emissions strand fewer assets (p. 34), and estimated stranded asset values range from $1 trillion to $18 trillion (Box 2, p. 24). Scenarios ”are not associated with probabilities” (p. 35)."},{"pdf":168,"printed":153,"ch":7,"text":"2. Translating the scenario into sector and firm metrics (3.3, pp. 36–41). Firm vulnerability depends on exposure, sensitivity and adaptive capacity (p. 36). Approaches use scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions (p. 38), sector classifications and financial network links (Battiston et al.), or production networks and input–output tables (Cahen-Fourot et al.) (p. 39).\n3. Turning that into financial risk (3.4, pp. 41–42): climate VaR, discounted cash flows into credit models. Three challenges: seeing the risk is not the same as acting on it (p. 41); individual institutions can hedge while the system cannot (p. 42); and the systemic, network effects of a climate-driven asset price shock are not yet modelled (p. 42). Box 4, the Dutch central bank (p. 36). A real example of a transition stress test: an abrupt $100 carbon tax plus a rapid renewable-technology shock, translated into macro effects through the NiGEM model, then into sector losses across 56 industries, then into financialsector portfolios. Result: losses of up to 11% of assets for insurers and up to 3% for banks, about a 4 percentage point reduction in Dutch banks' CET1 ratio. Quotable (p. 42), on hedging the system: ”reckoning climate-related risks can lead financial institutions to take rational actions that, while hedging them individually from a specific shock, do not hedge against the systemic risks posed by climate change.” Quotable (p. 43), risk vs uncertainty: ”The concept of 'risk' refers to something that has a calculable probability, whereas uncertainty refers to the possibility of outcomes that do not lend themselves to probability measurement.”\n\nThe chapter ends by calling for a second epistemological break (p. 43): from risk management to building the resilience of complex adaptive systems, with non-equilibrium models, sensitivity analyses and case studies as stopgaps (Box 5, p. 44).\n\n1.7 Prudential regulation (Section 4.1, pp. 50–53) ← closest to your thesis\n• Stress tests (p. 50). Climate scenarios can be added to stress tests. Physical-risk tests span decades and fit badly with short stress-test horizons; abrupt transition tests fit better. The practical use now is ”learning by doing” (p. 51). If material, systemic capital buffers could be applied.\n• Pillar 1 (minimum capital) (p. 52). Two ideas: a green supporting factor (lower requirements for green exposures) or a brown penalising factor (higher requirements for exposure to climate-exposed sectors). The authors say discussions are moving towards the brown penalising factor, because exposure to brown assets raises risk but it is ”not obvious” that green exposure lowers non-climate risk. Both need an agreed taxonomy of green and brown, which varies across jurisdictions. Also: ”greenness” or ”brownness” does not equal vulnerability to climate risk (p. 52).\n• Pillar 2 (supervisory review) (p. 52). Case-by-case extra capital if banks do not manage climate risk, plus governance expectations (UK PRA requires senior-management responsibility).\n• Pillar 3 (disclosure) (p. 52). Mandatory, consistent disclosure; example: Article 173 of the French energy transition law.\n• Emerging economies (p. 53). Some already act, though no capital-requirement measures yet: credit guidance such as Bangladesh's 5% green-lending allocation, differentiated"},{"pdf":169,"printed":154,"ch":7,"text":"reserve requirements in China and Lebanon.\n• Limits (p. 53). ”the role of prudential policy is to mitigate excessive financial risks … not to reconfigure the productive structures of the economy” while that is what climate needs; the EU SME supporting factor did not much change lending. So capital buffers can only partially hedge against green swans. Quotable (p. 53): prudential policy aims to ”mitigate excessive financial risks on the level of individual financial institutions and the financial system as a whole, not to reconfigure the productive structures of the economy”.\n\n1.8 The other propositions, briefly\nThe book's four proposals beyond carbon pricing (conclusion, p. 66): promote long-termism and sustainable finance (4.2); a larger role for fiscal policy, especially at the zero lower bound (4.3, not read); international monetary and financial cooperation, treating the carbon budget as a common pool resource after Ostrom (4.4, pp. 59–60); and integrating climate and natural capital into accounting (4.5, not read). In 4.2 the authors warn against confusing ESG tilts with hedging climate risk, because ESG filters measure a firm's impact on the environment, not the environment's impact on the firm (p. 54). In the conclusion they add: ”a new global financial crisis triggered by climate change would render central banks and financial supervisors powerless” (p. 65).\n\n1.9 Using this in your thesis\n• Justification for studying transition risk and capital adequacy: the book argues that regulators should integrate climate risk into stress tests and prudential rules (pp. 50–53), and that one-year, history-based capital models understate climate risk (p. 21).\n• Pillar-by-pillar frame for discussing what RBI and Basel could do (p. 52), and the brown-penalising vs green-supporting debate.\n• Emerging-economy evidence (p. 53) for India's context, including the point that no capital-requirement measures had yet been taken.\n• A model of a bank transition stress test (Box 4, p. 36) that you can compare with your design.\n• Caveat to state: the book is a policy and conceptual work, not an empirical study. Its numbers (Dutch stress test, stranded-asset ranges) are quoted from other studies. File flag. The file is the BIS book of January 2020, so the page numbers are the book's own and differ from PDF page numbers.\n\n2. Carney (2015), Breaking the Tragedy of the Horizon\nWhat it is. A dinner speech by Mark Carney, then Governor of the Bank of England and Chair of the Financial Stability Board, at Lloyd's of London on 29 September 2015. It is 16 printed pages and light on equations. Its importance is institutional: it is the founding text of central-bank climate-risk work and led directly to the FSB's TCFD (the Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures). Page numbers below are the speech's own printed pages (1 to 16), which match the PDF pages."},{"pdf":170,"printed":155,"ch":7,"text":"Read in full. Everything except the opening compliments to Lloyd's (pp. 1 to 2).\n\nThe argument in five moves\n1. Climate change is a horizon problem, not a commons problem (p. 4). The\nclassic environmental problem is the tragedy of the commons, solved by property rights and supply management. Climate is different: its worst costs fall beyond the horizon of every actor who could act.\n• Quotable (p. 4): ”Climate change is the Tragedy of the Horizon.”\n• Quotable (p. 4): ”imposing a cost on future generations that the current generation has no direct incentive to fix.”\n• The three horizons it lies beyond (p. 4): the business cycle, the political cycle, and the horizon of technocratic authorities such as central banks. Monetary policy looks 2 to 3 years ahead, financial stability about a decade (the credit cycle), and credit ratings only 3 to 5 years (fn 10).\n• Quotable (p. 4): ”once climate change becomes a defining issue for financial stability, it may already be too late.”\n2. Three channels to financial stability (pp. 5 to 6).\n• Physical risks (p. 5): impacts today on insurance liabilities and asset values from floods, storms and similar events.\n• Liability risks (p. 6): claims by those who suffered loss against those held responsible, hitting carbon extractors and emitters and their liability insurers. The long-tail analogy is asbestos, expected to cost US insurers about $85bn (p. 10). He notes courts have so far mostly rejected such cases (p. 10).\n• Transition risks (p. 6): the adjustment to a low-carbon economy, where changes in policy, technology and physical risks can force a reassessment of the value of a large range of assets.\n• Quotable (p. 6): ”The speed at which such re-pricing occurs is uncertain and could be decisive for financial stability.” He adds that there have already been examples of ”jump-to-distress pricing”.\n• Quotable (p. 6): risks are minimised ”if the transition begins early and follows a predictable path”.\n• Note for your thesis: Bolton et al. (Section 1) fold liability risk into transition risk, so the later literature uses a two-way split (physical, transition). Carney's three-way split is the original.\n3. Insurers show the physical-risk end (pp. 7 to 10). Weather-related loss events have tripled since the 1980s, and inflation-adjusted insured losses rose from about $10bn a year in the 1980s to about $50bn over the past decade (pp. 3 to 4). Insurers cope because they hold capital at a one-in-200-year standard and write short-term policies (p. 8), but modelled losses could be undervalued by as much as 50% if recent weather proves the new normal (p. 9). The policy lesson (p. 9) is that insurers' rational response (repricing, withdrawing cover) can create public-policy problems, such as the UK's Flood Re scheme.\n• Quotable (p. 8): ”past is not prologue and ... the catastrophic norms of the future"},{"pdf":171,"printed":156,"ch":7,"text":"can be seen in the tail risks of today.” This is the same argument Bolton et al. later make against history-based models.\n4. Transition risk and stranded assets (pp. 10 to 11). The IPCC carbon budget for a 2 degree world equals roughly one fifth to one third of proven oil, gas and coal reserves (p. 11), so most reserves would be ”stranded”, literally unburnable without carbon capture. Exposure is large: 19% of FTSE 100 companies are in extraction, a further 11% in power, chemicals, construction and industrials, and globally these two tiers hold around one third of equity and fixed-income assets (p. 11).\n• Quotable (p. 11): ”an abrupt resolution of the tragedy of horizons is in itself a financial stability risk.”\n• The mechanism he fears (p. 11): a sudden reassessment ”could potentially destabilise markets, spark a pro-cyclical crystallisation of losses and a persistent tightening of financial conditions.” This links straight to the Layer 4 spiral models.\n5. What policymakers should do (pp. 12 to 16).\n• Do not use prudential rules to push green finance. Quotable (p. 12): ”History shows the danger of attempting to use such changes in prudential rules ... for other ends.” He rejects a capital-regime tilt for green assets. Bolton et al. (p. 52) later reopen this debate with the brown penalising and green supporting factors, so you can cite the two as opposing positions.\n• Role of financial policymakers (p. 12): ”Financial policymakers will not drive the transition to a low-carbon economy.” Their job is resilience and efficient financing of the transition.\n• Disclosure first (pp. 12 to 14). Information about carbon intensity lets investors price risk. He uses Say's Law by analogy (p. 12): disclosure can create its own demand from analysts and investors. Disclosure should be consistent, comparable, reliable, clear and efficient (p. 14).\n• Smoothing the Minsky moment (p. 13): disclosure ”will help smooth price adjustments as opinions change, rather than concentrating them at a single climate 'Minsky moment'.”\n• Climate Disclosure Task Force (pp. 14 to 15): an industry-led group modelled on the FSB's Enhanced Disclosure Task Force for banks (2012). This became the TCFD.\n• Carbon price corridor (p. 15): governments give an indicative minimum and maximum carbon price path, rising over time. Even a low starting price ”holds great power” as a signal.\n• Stress testing (pp. 15 to 16): severe-but-plausible scenarios, as insurers already do. Quotable (p. 16): stress testing ”could act as a time machine, shining a light not just on today's risks, but on those that may otherwise lurk in the darkness for years to come.”\n• Closing line (p. 16): ”By managing what gets measured, we can break the Tragedy of the Horizon.”\n\nHow to use it in the thesis\n• Framing sentence for your introduction: the tragedy of the horizon explains why climate risk is absent from bank capital decisions that look out only a credit cycle ahead."},{"pdf":172,"printed":157,"ch":7,"text":"Cite p. 4.\n• Public sector banks: Carney's argument that a decade-long financial-stability horizon is too short applies even more to Indian PSBs, whose loans to power, steel and cement run well beyond that.\n• Link to Layer 3: he proposes stress testing as the tool (pp. 15 to 16); Borio, Drehmann and Tsatsaronis and the RBI stress-test documents are the methodology behind it.\n• Link to Layer 2: the carbon price corridor (p. 15) is the policy form of the Stern, Nordhaus and Weitzman price debate.\n\nFile flags\n• It is a speech, so the numbers (for example the 50% undervaluation, p. 9) are cited by him from secondary sources (S&P, Munich Re, IPCC). Quote the claim as Carney's, and go to the footnotes if you need the original.\n• It is from 2015 and predates the Paris Agreement. Carney's ”2 degree” language became\n1.5 to 2 degrees after 2015.\n\n3. Giglio, Kelly and Stroebel (2021), Climate Finance\nWhat it is. A review article in the Annual Review of Financial Economics, vol. 13, pp. 15 to 36 (the page numbers below are the journal's own, so you can cite them directly). It is the standard map of the field: Section 2 is theory (how to put climate risk into an asset-pricing model), Section 3 is evidence (is climate risk priced in stocks, bonds, housing and mortgages, and can you hedge it), Section 4 is a research agenda. The main text is about 17 pages; the rest is references. Read in full: the whole main text, pp. 15 to 32. Not read: the reference list (pp. 33 to 36). Where the notes below give a result from another paper (for example Bolton and Kacperczyk), it is the review's summary, not the original, so cite it as ”as reported in Giglio, Kelly and Stroebel (2021, p. X)” unless you read the original.\n\nWhy this paper matters for your thesis\nIt is the bridge between Layer 5 (asset-pricing vocabulary) and the climate-risk literature. It explains why a climate shock produces a risk premium, why the sign of that premium is disputed, and what the empirical record says. It is also silent on banks and credit, which is the gap your thesis fills (see the last part of this section).\n\nSection 1. The framing (pp. 16 to 17)\n• Two climate risks are priced: physical risk (direct impairment of productive assets) and transition risk (risk to cash flows from a move to a low-carbon economy) (p. 16).\n• The research design shared across papers (p. 16): assets are differentially exposed (houses near the sea, coal companies), and attention to climate risk varies over time. Exposure differences plus attention changes identify pricing.\n• Quotable (p. 16, fn 1): climate finance ”concentrate[s] more squarely on the covariance properties of asset payoffs with climate change as a systematic risk factor”, unlike the"},{"pdf":173,"printed":158,"ch":7,"text":"older ESG-performance literature. This is a clean sentence for separating your topic from ESG studies.\n\nSection 2. Theory: one toy model, two stories (pp. 17 to 24)\nThe model (p. 17). A representative-agent Lucas-tree economy with three equations: consumption growth (Eq. 1), expected growth with persistence ρ (Eq. 2), and the probability λ of the shock J (Eq. 3). J is the only shock: it takes the value −ξ with probability λ and 0 otherwise. Everything depends on what you take J to be. Story A: J is a climate disaster (Section 2.1, p. 18). Uncertainty is about the climate path itself (a tipping point; Weitzman, Barro, Wagner and Weitzman). Climate damage arrives when consumption is already low. Parameter φ governs whether the shock also lowers future growth (φ > 0) or the economy partly rebounds through adaptation (φ < 0). Story B: J is a shock to economic activity (Section 2.2, pp. 18 to 19). Uncertainty is about growth, and damages scale with consumption (the Nordhaus DICE specification). Higher growth means more emissions, so more damage later. Damages are larger when the economy is doing well. Common implication (p. 19): in both stories an asset exposed to climate risk loses value when an adverse climate shock hits. The sign of the risk premium flips (Section 2.3, pp. 19 to 21). This is the central insight of the theory section.\n• Story A (p. 19): exposed assets have low payoffs when damage is high, so they need positive risk premia. Mitigation investments pay off in bad states, so they have negative risk premia: they are insurance. They should be discounted below the risk-free rate, which makes the social cost of carbon high (p. 20).\n• Story B (p. 20): mitigation pays off in good times when marginal utility is low, so it carries a positive risk premium, is discounted above the risk-free rate, and the social cost of carbon is low.\n• Quotable (p. 20, quoting Lemoine 2021, p. 29): under conventional damage specifications ”emission reductions increase future consumption by a larger amount when future consumption is otherwise high”, which ”makes emission reductions seem like an especially risky investment”.\n• Term structure (p. 20): with persistent shocks (ρ > 0) and long-run risk (φ > 0), the premium grows more extreme with horizon. With φ < 0 (quick recovery) the premium reverts toward zero as the horizon lengthens.\n• What the data say (pp. 20 to 21): equities and real estate have positive risk premia that decline with horizon. The authors read this as favouring Story A with φ < 0: a climate-disaster model plus adaptation.\n• Quotable (p. 21): the debate over discount rates ”can in large part be traced to different assumptions about the nature of the shocks that mitigation investments are hedging”. This is the bridge to Layer 2: it explains why Stern, Nordhaus and Weitzman disagree. Preferences (Section 2.4, p. 21). Stern (2007) set pure time preference at effectively zero on ethical grounds; Nordhaus (2007) and Weitzman (2007) use positive rates consistent with"},{"pdf":174,"printed":159,"ch":7,"text":"observed behaviour (p. 21). Epstein-Zin preferences raise the value of acting early and imply declining carbon-price paths (Daniel, Litterman and Wagner, p. 21). Model uncertainty (Section 2.5, pp. 21 to 24).\n• Rational expectations give agents an unrealistic knowledge of the probability law. Quotable (p. 22, quoting Hansen 2014, p. 950): ”it is often not clear what information should be presumed on the part of economic agents, how they should use it, and how much confidence they have in that use.”\n• Figure 1 (p. 22) shows how widely climate-model temperature projections diverge.\n• Lemoine (2021) shows that accounting for model uncertainty raises the social cost of carbon, ”by an order of magnitude” in models with recursive preferences (p. 23).\n• Learning is a long-run risk: Bayesian belief updates are permanent shocks and are especially costly for recursive-preference agents (p. 23). This motivates Engle et al.'s news-based measure.\n• Ambiguity aversion adds roughly 60 to 70% to the cost of carbon (Barnett, Brock and Hansen 2020, p. 24).\n• Footnote 2 (p. 23): the climate beta (Dietz, Gollier and Kessler 2018) is the covariance of consumption and the marginal social cost of emissions. Use this definition if your thesis uses the term.\n\nSection 3. Evidence: what is priced? (pp. 24 to 32)\nTwo framing points (pp. 24 to 25).\n• Physical and transition risks need not occur together and can move in opposite directions: a carbon tax is a transition-risk realization but reduces future physical risk (p. 24). There are winners and losers in both (coal vs renewables; coastal vs cold-region farmland) (p. 24).\n• Quotable (p. 25): attention to climate risk is recent, so ”while climate risk may be priced in asset markets today, it might not have been 10 or 15 years ago.” With a short time series it is hard to estimate the premium. 3.1.1 Equities (pp. 25 to 26).\n• Bolton and Kacperczyk (2020): higher-emission US firms trade at a discount; a one standard deviation rise in emissions is associated with roughly a 2% per annum higher expected return (p. 25). They trace part of it to exclusionary screening by institutions.\n• Hsu, Li and Tsou (2020): a pollution premium linked to environmental-policy uncertainty (p. 25).\n• Engle et al. (2020): high E-Score stocks earn more in periods of bad climate news (pp. 25 to 26). Choi, Gao and Jiang (2020): carbon-intensive stocks underperform in abnormally warm weather (p. 26).\n• Barnett (2019), an event study of policy-likelihood increases, finds lower prices for firms exposed to policy risk (p. 26). This is an application of the MacKinlay method you read in Layer 5.\n• Options market: downside protection is costlier for carbon-intensive firms, especially when attention is high (Ilhan, Sautner and Vilkov, p. 26)."},{"pdf":175,"printed":160,"ch":7,"text":"• Krueger, Sautner and Starks (2020) survey: 39% of investors were working to reduce portfolio carbon footprints (p. 25). 3.1.2 Fixed income (p. 26).\n• Municipal bonds are the clean physical-risk test, because municipalities cannot relocate. Painter (2020): at-issue yields are higher for counties with large expected sea-levelrise losses, concentrated in long-dated bonds and absent at short maturities (p. 26). Goldsmith-Pinkham et al.: equivalent to a 3 to 8% fall in the present value of local cash flows (p. 26).\n• Corporate bonds: Huynh and Xia (2021) use the Engle climate-news covariance; bonds that do well on bad climate news have lower returns (p. 26).\n• Green bonds: about 6 basis points lower after-tax yield at issue (Baker et al. 2018, p. 26), attributed to investors with a non-pecuniary taste (p. 27). 3.1.3 Hedging (pp. 27 to 29). Many climate risks are too distant for derivatives or insurance to hedge (p. 27), so investors must build hedge portfolios themselves.\n• Engle et al. (2020): follow the Black-Scholes-Merton logic that a dynamic strategy replicates an infeasible contract (p. 27). Build a climate-news index from Wall Street Journal text (Figure 2, p. 27), then use factor-mimicking portfolios of US stocks that rise on bad climate news. Out-of-sample correlation with news innovations is 20 to 30% (p. 28).\n• Quotable (p. 28): ”the risk premium of any nontradable risk (like climate change risk) is the expected excess return of the corresponding hedging portfolio.” This is the Cochrane logic from Layer 5 (a risk premium is compensation for covariance with the discount factor).\n• Warning (p. 28): a negative-premium hedge portfolio can show a positive average return over the last 10 to 15 years because bad climate news dominated that window, so you would estimate the wrong sign. Be careful reading short-sample average returns of green or brown portfolios.\n• Alekseev et al. (2021): use mutual-fund managers' trades after local extreme-weather events to build a hedge (p. 29).\n3.2 Housing and mortgages (pp. 29 to 32).\n• Identification problem (p. 29): beach access is both an amenity and a flood exposure, so a cross-section cannot separate them. The fix is time variation in attention.\n• Giglio et al. (2021): NOAA 6-foot sea-level-rise maps plus a Zillow-listing attention index. A doubling of climate attention is associated with a relative 2.4% decline in flood-zone prices (p. 30). Rents do not fall, which shows the effect is about expected future cash flows, not current utility (p. 31).\n• Bernstein, Gustafson and Lewis (2019): exposed houses sell at about a 14.7% discount for 1 foot of sea-level rise, 13.8% for 2 to 3 feet, 4.4% for 6 feet (p. 31).\n• Baldauf, Garlappi and Yannelis (2020): pricing depends on whether the local population believes in climate change (p. 31). Murfin and Spiegel (2020) find a much smaller effect, so identification matters (p. 31, fn 6).\n• Hurricane Sandy studies (Ortega and Taspinar; Gibson et al.; Eichholtz et al.): flood-zone properties see permanent relative price falls even when undamaged (p. 31)."},{"pdf":176,"printed":161,"ch":7,"text":"• Wildfire: 776,000 US homes with $221bn reconstruction cost at extreme risk (CoreLogic, p. 31).\n• Mortgages (p. 32): disasters raise default (Issler et al.), and perceived climate risk affects banks' choice to securitize (Ouazad and Kahn). This is the closest the review gets to banks.\n\nSection 4. Research agenda (p. 32)\n• Better asset-level exposure measures from disclosure, satellite imagery and text.\n• Quotable (p. 32): whether climate risk threatens financial stability ”depends, to a large extent, on the degree of concentration of these risks in the portfolios of financial institutions and investors.” Measuring that concentration is named as an open agenda.\n\nHow to use it in the thesis\n• Gap statement: this review documents pricing in equities, bonds, housing and mortgages, almost all for the US. It has no evidence on bank capital, loan books, or emergingmarket public sector banks. State that gap, citing p. 32.\n• Methods to borrow: (1) exposure times attention design (pp. 16, 29 to 30); (2) eventstudy logic (p. 26, Layer 5); (3) climate-news hedging factor (pp. 27 to 28) if you test bank-stock sensitivity to climate news in India.\n• Theory sentence: the sign of the climate risk premium depends on whether the shock is a climate disaster or an economic-activity shock (pp. 19 to 20). Useful when you explain why the discount rate matters (Layer 2).\n• Link to Layer 5: the Stroebel-Wurgler survey respondents (physical risk top at 30 years, regulatory risk top at 5 years) are the practitioner counterpart of the physicalversus-transition split here.\n\nFile flags\n• US-centric evidence throughout; nothing on India.\n• The review is dated 2021. The papers it calls working papers (for example Alekseev et al., Giglio et al.) have since been published or revised, so check the final versions before citing numbers.\n• The full texts of the underlying studies were not read, only this review's summaries.\n\n4. Monasterolo (2020), Climate Change and the Financial System\nWhat it is. A review in the Annual Review of Resource Economics, vol. 12, pp. 299 to 320 (the page numbers below are the journal's own). Irene Monasterolo works on climate-financial risk using the complexity and network approach, and the paper is partly a showcase for her group's CLIMAFIN toolkit (Battiston, Mandel and Monasterolo). It is the closest paper in Layer 6 to a bank-capital question, because it covers the green supporting factor and brown penalising factor, climate VaR, and network stress tests of banks."},{"pdf":177,"printed":162,"ch":7,"text":"Read in full: the main text, pp. 299 to 316. Not read: the reference list (pp. 316 to 320). The cited results below are her summaries of other papers, so cite them as ”Monasterolo (2020, p. X) reporting ...” unless you open the original. Bias to keep in mind: much of Sections 5 and 6 reviews the author's own co-authored work (Battiston et al. 2017, 2019; Monasterolo and Battiston 2016). Treat it as one school (network and complexity economics), not neutral consensus.\n\nSection 1 and 2. Why climate risk reached the policy agenda (pp. 300 to 302)\n• Paris Agreement Article 2.1(c) commits to ”making finance flows consistent with a pathway towards low greenhouse gas emissions” (p. 300). IPCC estimate: US$2.4 trillion a year (2.5% of world economy) for the energy system alone by 2035 for 1.5 degrees (p. 300).\n• The Battiston et al. (2017) climate stress test found pension and investment funds hold circa 40% of equity portfolios in assets that could become stranded in a disorderly transition (p. 300).\n• Two limitations (pp. 300 to 301): (1) traditional pricing and risk models are ”backward looking and consider normal distributions, perfect markets, equilibrium, and lack of arbitrage” (p. 300); (2) climate economics models (IAMs and SSPs) ”do not consider finance and its complexity” (p. 301).\n• Timeline (pp. 301 to 302): Carney's 2015 speech (Section 2 of these notes), the Bank of England's 2016 definitions of physical and transition risk (Batten et al.), the TCFD (2017), the NGFS created in 2017 and by 2019 with 34 members representing 50% of global emissions and two-thirds of systemically relevant financial institutions (p. 302), and NGFS's 2019 recommendation of climate stress tests. The Dutch central bank scenarios are cited on p. 302. Useful as a dated chronology for your literature review.\n\nSection 3. Risk drivers and transmission channels (pp. 302 to 307)\nPhysical risk (pp. 302 to 304). Defined as damage to physical assets, natural capital and human lives, reducing productive capacity and GDP (p. 302). She lists: destruction of productive capital, falling property values (affecting banks and insurers), wildfires, water stress, biodiversity loss, soil erosion (pp. 303 to 304). Data point (p. 303): climate-related extremes accounted for €456bn, 83% of monetary losses over 1980 to 2017 in the European Economic Area, with only about 35% insured. Compound risks and tipping points can create domino effects (p. 304). Transition risk (p. 304).\n• Definition (p. 304): ”the economic and financial losses arising from the revaluation of carbon-intensive and low-carbon assets induced by a sudden change in policy and/or regulation that cannot be fully anticipated by financial actors.”\n• Drivers: technological shocks, policy and regulatory shocks (including a carbon tax, a green supporting factor or a brown penalising factor), and sudden changes in ”climate sentiments” (p. 304).\n• Orderly vs disorderly transition (p. 304). Orderly: policies early and coordinated, investors price them in. Disorderly: late and sudden, so unanticipated shocks hit market share and asset values. This distinction is the backbone of NGFS scenarios."},{"pdf":178,"printed":163,"ch":7,"text":"• Benchmark argument (p. 305): because asset managers follow benchmarks (Greenwald and Stiglitz), and the benchmark stays carbon-intensive, stranded assets are not avoided in time. Policy forecast errors (Paris, US withdrawal, the 2018 Italian elections) show that even the average market view can be wrong (p. 305). Transmission channels to banks (Section 3.3, pp. 305 to 307), following Dunz et al. (2019). This is the most thesis-relevant passage in the paper.\n• Green supporting factor (GSF) (p. 305, Figure 1): a cut in Basel III capital requirements for loans to low-carbon firms. Lower risk weights raise the bank's capital adequacy ratio, lower interest rates for low-carbon firms, raise their demand and profits, and raise the green share of bank loans. It cascades through interest rates, prices, demand and loan exposure.\n• Carbon tax (p. 306, Figure 2): raises production costs of carbon-intensive firms, passes to households through mark-up pricing, cuts profitability and ability to service loans, raises non-performing loans, and tightens bank lending conditions for those firms.\n• Climate sentiments (p. 306, Figure 3): if the bank anticipates a carbon tax, it raises rates on carbon-intensive firms in advance.\n• Quotable (p. 307): climate sentiments ”work as an indirect brown penalizing factor”.\n• You can cite these three figures as a ready-made conceptual diagram of how a climate shock reaches the capital adequacy ratio. They are copyrighted, so redraw rather than copy.\n\nSection 4. Why standard models fail (pp. 307 to 308)\n• IAM and cost-benefit work ”neglected the role of finance”, so scenarios judged achievable may fail for lack of investor commitment (p. 307).\n• Five features of climate risk (pp. 307 to 308):\n1. Nonlinearity: not normally distributed. Example (p. 307, citing Ackerman): the 2003 Western European summer was 5.4 standard deviations above the 1864 to 2000 mean temperature, and a similar heatwave recurred in Eastern Europe in 2010, so a normal distribution cannot be right.\n2. Deep uncertainty: tail events and tipping points (Weitzman), and cost-benefit results that depend on utility function, growth and the discount rate (Stern, Pindyck) (p. 307). This ties to Layer 2.\n3. Forward-looking nature: impacts over two decades or more, versus fund managers' three-month horizons (p. 307). A restatement of Carney's horizon problem.\n4. Complexity: emergent system behaviour that cannot be derived from summing individual behaviour (p. 308).\n5. Endogeneity and circularity: uncertainty about climate policy shapes investment and thereby affects whether policy targets are met, giving multiple equilibria (p. 308).\n• Network and complexity models add: feedback loops and cascades, tipping points, and policy path dependency (p. 308)."},{"pdf":179,"printed":164,"ch":7,"text":"Section 5. CLIMAFIN and applications (pp. 308 to 313)\n• Evidence that climate risk is not yet priced (p. 309): the green-bond premium findings differ by contract type and definition (Karpf and Mandel; Alessi et al.; Zerbib); the catastrophe-bond multiple fell despite rising disasters (Morana and Sbrana); stock markets reacted mildly to the Paris Agreement announcement for carbon-intensive indices (Monasterolo and de Angelis 2020); Ramelli et al. found the opposite after Trump's election. Compare with Giglio et al. (Section 3 of these notes), who report priced risk in several markets.\n• CLIMAFIN components (p. 309): classify exposure in climate-policy-relevant sectors (CPRS); price climate risk into equity, bonds and loans; build climate spread and climate VaR; run a network climate stress test.\n• Six steps (p. 310): (1) investor information set, (2) risk-management strategy, (3) portfolio of defaultable contracts, (4) macro and sector trajectories at issuer level, (5) valuation model conditioned on climate shock scenarios, (6) definitions of the two metrics.\n• Definitions to quote (p. 310): the climate spread is ”the change in spread of a corporate or sovereign bond contract conditional to a given climate policy shock scenario”; the climate VaR is the ”worst-case loss” under future climate shock scenarios at a given confidence level.\n• Sovereign bonds of OECD countries (pp. 310 to 311, Table 1): a sudden carbon price consistent with 2 degrees hits countries with large fossil shares of gross value added (Australia, Norway) hardest; countries with growing renewables (Austria) gain. Table 1 shows shocks varying by model (WITCH versus GCAM), with one cell of about −17% on bond value (the table is partly garbled in the text extraction, so check figures in the PDF before citing).\n• Development banks (p. 311): Caribbean Development Bank top 100 projects: about 60% highly exposed to climate physical and transition risks, about 47% increase vulnerability, only 12% increase resilience (Figure 4). Recovery rate on loans near zero after a sovereign default such as Grenada, 2004 to 2006, after Hurricane Ivan.\n• Chinese policy banks (pp. 311 to 312, Table 2): 199 overseas energy loans,\nUS$228bn, 63 countries, 2000 to 2018. Largest negative shocks of 4% to 22% of portfolios; with average leverage of 12, even an average shock of about 10% could put the banks in financial distress (p. 312). This is the nearest quantification to a bank-capital argument in the paper.\n• European insurers' sovereign bonds (pp. 312 to 313): impact of a disorderly\ntransition is ”moderate but non-negligible”.\n\nSection 6. Network models and the climate stress test (pp. 313 to 315)\n• Why networks (p. 313): they capture second-round (indirect) effects, diversification across counterparties and feedback to the real economy, which standard stress tests miss.\n• Three drivers of systemic risk in networks (pp. 313 to 314): interconnectedness, leverage with endogenous recovery rate, and mispricing of traded contracts.\n• Battiston et al. (2017) climate stress test (p. 314): exposure to CPRS found from NACE 4-digit classification; first- and second-round losses calculated with the DebtRank algorithm; climate VaR for the 20 most exposed EU and US banks under green or fossil investment strategies (Figure 5). Exposures of pension funds and investment funds reach"},{"pdf":180,"printed":165,"ch":7,"text":"43% and 45% of equity portfolios, amplified by mutual holdings (p. 314).\n• Banco de Mexico application by Roncoroni et al. (2019) uses the NEVA network valuation with endogenous recovery rates (pp. 314 to 315).\n• Stolbova et al. (2018) assess carbon tax, green QE and GSF on Eurozone institutions through direct and indirect chains (p. 315), but without behavioural responses; later multilayer agent-based work (Naqvi and Monasterolo) adds them.\n\nConclusion (pp. 315 to 316)\n• Quotable (p. 315): climate risk ”differs from other sources of risk because it is forward looking and global ... and characterized by deep uncertainty, nonlinearity, and endogeneity.”\n• Quotable (p. 316): ”the assessment of climate-related financial risks should be considered as a public good and should be mainstreamed in economic and financial assessments and in policy evaluation.”\n\nHow to use it in the thesis\n• Transmission-channel diagram: use Section 3.3 (pp. 305 to 307) to explain how a carbon tax or GSF reaches non-performing loans and the capital adequacy ratio. This is the conceptual spine for linking climate transition risk to CAR in Indian PSBs.\n• Methods lineage: DebtRank and network valuation (p. 314) link to Layer 4 (EisenbergNoe, Acemoglu et al.). You can describe your own approach as simpler (regression on bank data) and cite this paper for the more elaborate network alternative.\n• Leverage point (p. 312): with leverage of 12, a roughly 10% shock threatens solvency. PSBs have comparable asset-to-equity ratios, so you can adapt the argument to Indian numbers.\n• Contradicting views to cite: Carney (2015, p. 12) opposes using capital rules for climate, while this paper models the GSF (p. 305) as a policy lever. Use both when discussing the capital-regime debate.\n\nFile flags\n• Table 1 and Table 2 numbers extracted from the PDF are partly garbled (columns separated). Verify against the PDF before quoting any figure.\n• Page numbers: the text extraction places page numbers at the foot of each page, so p. 300 is the introduction. I have checked that mapping against the running footers.\n• No India evidence at all; the empirical cases are Europe, OECD, Caribbean and China.\n\n5. Zhou, Endendijk and Botzen (2023), Financial Sector Impacts of\nClimate Change Risks\nWhat it is. A review in the Annual Review of Resource Economics, vol. 15, pp. 233 to 256 (the page numbers below are the journal's own). It surveys the evidence on how natural disasters and physical climate risk affect insurance, banking, equity markets, bond markets"},{"pdf":181,"printed":166,"ch":7,"text":"and international financial flows. The authors are Dutch environmental economists (Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam), and the paper is open access. Read in full: the main text, pp. 233 to 250. Not read: the reference list and the Supplemental Appendix tables A1 to A5, which list the individual studies and are not in your file. Scope warning (p. 234, fn 1): the review deliberately ”exclude[s] transition risks” and sends the reader to Monasterolo (2020) and Semieniuk et al. (2021). So it is the physical-risk companion to the other papers in this layer. Use it to show the physical side of your thesis background, and the methods used to estimate bank outcomes. Do not cite it for transition-risk claims.\n\nSection 1 and 2. Framework (pp. 234 to 236)\n• Why the financial sector matters (p. 234): it channels savings to investment, lets risk be traded and pooled, and finances post-disaster recovery. Disasters threaten all three.\n• Two channels (p. 235, Figure 1): a physical channel (direct and indirect losses worsen balance sheets of firms, households, insurers, banks and governments) and an expectation channel (disasters change risk perception and uncertainty about future profitability, raising risk premiums and volatility).\n• Bank-specific transmission (p. 235): ”asset and collateral value depreciation, deposit withdrawals, and nonperforming loans” on the bank side; recovery credit demand rises at the same time. Whether the sector can meet the surge in demand depends on ”disaster type and intensity, capital abundance, financial regulations (e.g., on own capital and mortgage securitization), and levels of socioeconomic development” (p. 235).\n• You can redraw Figure 1 as a short diagram in your thesis; it is a compact way to separate physical from expectation effects.\n\nSection 3.1. Insurance (pp. 236 to 239)\n• Insurers are ”generally viewed as the part of the financial sector that is most directly affected by climate change” (p. 236). Most disaster insurance exists in developed countries (p. 236).\n• Gaining-from-loss hypothesis (p. 236): disasters can raise demand for insurance and profits. Mixed evidence: negative in the US, positive in Japan (Wang and Kutan, p. 238).\n• Methods (pp. 236 to 238): statistical claims models (backward-looking, with limited local data); catastrophe models that simulate hazard, exposure and vulnerability; agent-based models that add adaptation (Haer et al.), who find that ignoring adaptation overestimates future flood risk (p. 238).\n• Numbers: Botzen and Bouwer: Dutch hail damage up to +33% by 2050 (p. 237).\nKunreuther et al.: Florida annual premiums rise from $13bn (1990) to $32bn (2040) (p. 237). Hudson et al.: average EU flood premium may double 2015 to 2055 (p. 238). Tesselaar et al.: severe climate change could make insurance unaffordable in some regions and in hard reinsurance markets (p. 238)."},{"pdf":182,"printed":167,"ch":7,"text":"Section 3.2. Banking (pp. 239 to 241) — the most relevant part for you\nMechanisms (p. 239). Banks' balance sheets worsen through lower asset and collateral values; branches and data centres can be damaged; borrowers' credit standing falls; but recovery borrowing rises. Methods (p. 239). Most studies use panel data for disaster-hit areas with ”panel fixed effects, difference-in-differences, and the system generalized method of moments estimator”, but ”the disaster impacts derived from such methods are mostly short-term local effects” (p. 239). Quotable and a good description of the standard toolkit, which you can cite for your own econometric design. Forward-looking exceptions (p. 239): Bos et al. (2018) combine difference-in-differences with a dynamic credit-rationing model; Lamperti et al. (2019) calibrate a global agent-based integrated assessment model with a banking sector; and climate stress testing by central banks (Hallegatte et al. 2022; Batten et al. 2016). The authors note stress tests look at ”profitability, solvency, and liquidity” (p. 239). Bank stability (3.2.2, p. 240). Measures: distance-to-default, probability of insolvency, return on assets, equity-to-assets (p. 240). Distance-to-default (fn 2, p. 240) is ”the number of standard deviations that a bank's return on assets has to drop below its expected value before equity is depleted”.\n• Klomp (2014): disasters reduced distance-to-default for commercial banks in emerging economies, but not in advanced economies (p. 240).\n• Chen and Chang (2021): effects work indirectly through financial risk and shrink as income rises (p. 240).\n• Lamperti et al.: climate change raises the frequency of banking crises by 26 to 148% (p. 240).\n• US evidence is mixed: Noth and Schüwer find weakened stability within two to three years; Blickle et al. find only extreme disasters (90th percentile damage) matter, and not for solvency; Brei et al. find no rise in defaults or fall in capital in the Eastern Caribbean after hurricanes (p. 240). Credit supply (3.2.3, pp. 240 to 241). Evidence is ”mixed” (p. 240).\n• Falls: Eastern Caribbean banks cut lending after deposit withdrawals (Brei et al.); Thai bank lending fell for up to 22 months after the Indian Ocean tsunami (Nguyen and Wilson); firms outside Kobe's earthquake area got less credit from damaged home banks (Hosono et al.) (p. 240).\n• Rises: multi-market and better-capitalised local banks in the US lent more after disasters (p. 240). Firms in flooded German counties connected to banks in unflooded counties borrowed 16% more (Koetter et al., p. 241). How banks fund recovery lending (3.2.4, p. 241). Four channels: selling liquid assets and government securities; mortgage-backed securitization; reallocating capital from unaffected markets; interbank access. Quotable on the capital point (p. 241): positive recovery-lending effects ”are mostly observed among banks with high bank capital ratios” (Schüwer et al.). Public support must be well designed: Ouazad and Kahn find adverse selection into securitizing"},{"pdf":183,"printed":168,"ch":7,"text":"mortgages after disasters (p. 241), and Uchida et al. find capital injections into Japanese banks after Tohoku weakened firm selection (p. 241). Authors' bank conclusion (p. 249): disasters ”tend to reduce bank stability and increase the credit supply of banks in developed countries such as the United States and Germany, but ... lower credit supply in developing countries such as Thailand and the Eastern Caribbean islands.” Financial regulation and public support that ”ensure sufficient bank capital and reduce risky lending facilitate economic recovery.”\n\nSection 3.3. Equity markets (pp. 241 to 243)\n• Theory (p. 241): rare-disaster asset-pricing models (Barro; Wachter) explain the equity premium puzzle and excess volatility, and disasters raise the perceived disaster probability and required returns. This is the same stochastic discount factor logic as Layer 5 (Cochrane).\n• Method (p. 242): ”An event study approach is widely used”, resting on the efficient market hypothesis. GARCH models handle time-varying variance (p. 242). This reconnects to MacKinlay (1997).\n• Wealth effect (pp. 242 to 243): mixed. No aggregate effect in Australia, US or Japan (Worthington; Wang and Kutan); but earthquakes cut market value by 6 to 12% in 21 countries (Scholtens and Voorhorst, p. 242); Cambridge hypothetical trillion-dollar catastrophes cut equity indices 6 to 20% (pp. 242 to 243). Effects last days to three months for directly exposed firms (p. 243).\n• Risk effect (p. 243): volatility rises; major hurricanes, floods and extreme temperatures doubled the volatility of local firms' stocks (Bourdeau-Brien and Kryzanowski).\n• Spillovers (p. 243): contagion to neighbours and trading partners after Katrina, the 2004 tsunami, Tohoku and Sichuan.\n\nSection 3.4. Bond markets (pp. 243 to 246)\n• Credit spreads in rare-disaster real business cycle models rise with disaster risk (Gourio, p. 244).\n• Sovereign: one unit more climate vulnerability raises borrowing costs by 1.17% on average (Kling et al.); Klusak et al. find climate-induced sovereign downgrades as early as 2030 and extra annual interest payments of $22 to 33bn under RCP 2.6 and $137 to 205bn under RCP 8.5 (p. 245).\n• Municipal: exposed municipalities pay more, particularly for long maturities (Painter, Goldsmith-Pinkham et al.) (p. 245).\n• Corporate: climate vulnerability restricts access to finance and raises debt costs (Kling et al.: 71 countries, 1999 to 2017, p. 245). Insurers liquidating bonds to pay claims drive up spreads (Massa and Zhang, p. 245).\n• CAT bonds: Katrina raised reinsurer cost of capital 15 to 20%; hurricane seasonality explains about 47% of CAT-bond yield fluctuations (Dieckmann; Herrmann and Hibbeln, pp. 245 to 246)."},{"pdf":184,"printed":169,"ch":7,"text":"Section 3.5. International financial flows (pp. 246 to 247)\n• Low- and middle-income countries: remittances and aid rise after disasters, but aid covers only about 3% of damage; net bank lending and FDI fall (p. 247).\n• High-income countries: FDI falls in the short run and rises in the medium run, in manufacturing, construction and tourism (p. 247).\n• Households in China and India with remittances had higher adaptive capacity (Banerjee et al., p. 246). This is the only India-related item in the paper.\n\nSection 4 and 5. Discussion and gaps (pp. 247 to 250)\n• Quotable (p. 247): ”most studies of the impacts of natural disasters focus on developed countries with comparatively advanced financial sectors”; ”few studies ... zoom in on specific developing countries”, mainly because of ”the lack of financial and disaster data”.\n• The limited developing-country evidence ”generally shows more severe adverse impacts” (p. 247). Useful support for arguing that Indian PSBs may be more exposed than USbased estimates suggest, though it is not evidence about India itself.\n• Methodological limits of the statistical approach (p. 248): effects are ”local and sector-specific without considering inter-region and inter-sector interactions”; they assume linear impacts when climate effects are nonlinear (they cite Monasterolo 2020); they cannot capture long-run adaptation; and cross-country studies suffer from heterogeneity bias.\n• Compound and correlated hazards (pp. 249 to 250): most evidence is based on\nsingle events, so it may understate future impacts. The authors call for forward-looking computational models (p. 249 to 250).\n\nHow to use it in the thesis\n• Physical-risk backdrop: one paragraph arguing that physical climate shocks already affect bank stability and credit supply, citing p. 240 to 241, then saying your thesis focuses on transition risk, which the authors explicitly exclude (p. 234, fn 1).\n• Econometric design: the review describes the bank-level toolkit (panel fixed effects, difference-in-differences, system GMM; p. 239) and the stability outcomes (distance-todefault, ROA, equity-to-assets; p. 240). Capital adequacy is a natural additional outcome variable.\n• Link to Layer 5: event-study logic (p. 242), rare-disaster asset pricing (p. 241).\n• Contrast with Giglio et al. (Section 3): both show climate risk is priced in some markets and not in others. Zhou et al. emphasise heterogeneity by income, regulation and capital (p. 249); Giglio et al. emphasise attention and exposure.\n• Gap statement: no bank-level evidence for India or for public sector banks, and no transition-risk evidence on bank capital (p. 247).\n\nFile flags\n• Review of other studies: cite individual results as ”as reported in Zhou, Endendijk and Botzen (2023, p. X)” unless you read the originals.\n• Physical risk only; mostly US, Europe and Japan."},{"pdf":185,"printed":170,"ch":7,"text":"• The appendix tables listing the underlying studies were not read.\n\n6. Master quotables and how Layer 6 feeds the thesis\nTwelve quotables with page numbers\nPage numbers are the printed pages of each source (Bolton: the book's own pagination; Carney: speech pages 1 to 16; the three journal reviews: the journal's pages). Check each wording against your PDF before it goes into the thesis. Table 7.3. Master quotables and how Layer 6 feeds the thesis: Twelve quotables with page numbers\n\nSource and page\n\nQuote\n\nUse it for\n\nCarney (2015), p. 4\n\n”Climate change is the Tragedy of the\nHorizon.”\n\nOpening framing: why\nclimate risk is missed\n\nCarney (2015), p. 4\n\n”once climate change becomes a defining\nJustifying a\nissue for financial stability, it may already forward-looking study now\nbe too late.”\n\nCarney (2015), p.\n\n”an abrupt resolution of the tragedy of\nhorizons is in itself a financial stability\nrisk.”\n\nWhy transition risk is a\nstability issue\n\nCarney (2015), p.\n\n”History shows the danger of attempting\nto use such changes in prudential rules ...\nfor other ends.”\n\nThe case against a green\ncapital tilt\n\nBolton et al. (2020), ”climate-related risks will remain largely\np. 1\nunhedgeable as long as system-wide\naction is not undertaken.”\n\nBolton et al. (2020), ”green swan events cannot be captured by Limits of VaR and p. 21 traditional risk management.” history-based models\n\nGiglio, Kelly and\nStroebel (2021), p.\n\nThe discount-rate debate ”can in large\npart be traced to different assumptions\nabout the nature of the shocks that\nmitigation investments are hedging”.\n\nGiglio, Kelly and\nStroebel (2021), p.\n\n”the risk premium of any nontradable risk Bridge to Cochrane and\n(like climate change risk) is the expected\nfactor models\nexcess return of the corresponding\nhedging portfolio.”\n\nGiglio, Kelly and\nStroebel (2021), p.\n\nFinancial stability impact ”depends, to a\nlarge extent, on the degree of\nconcentration of these risks in the\nportfolios of financial institutions and\ninvestors.”\n\nMonasterolo (2020), Transition risks are ”the economic and\np. 304\nfinancial losses arising from the\nrevaluation of carbon-intensive and\nlow-carbon assets induced by a sudden\nchange in policy and/or regulation that\ncannot be fully anticipated by financial\nactors.”\n\n170\n\nWhy bank-level hedging is\nnot enough\n\nLinking Layer 2 (Stern,\nNordhaus, Weitzman) to\nasset pricing\n\nGap statement: bank\nexposure concentration\n\nFormal definition of\ntransition risk"},{"pdf":186,"printed":171,"ch":7,"text":"Source and page\n\nQuote\n\nUse it for\n\nMonasterolo (2020), Standard models are ”backward looking\nCritique of conventional\np. 300\nand consider normal distributions, perfect risk models\nmarkets, equilibrium, and lack of\narbitrage”.\n\nZhou, Endendijk\nand Botzen (2023),\np. 247\n\n”few studies ... zoom in on specific\nGap statement:\ndeveloping countries” because of ”the lack emerging-market banks\nof financial and disaster data”.\n\nTwo spare quotes: Monasterolo (2020, p. 316) that climate-related financial risks ”should be considered as a public good”; Zhou et al. (2023, p. 239) that disaster impacts from standard panel methods ”are mostly short-term local effects”.\n\nThe four debates you can stage in your literature review\n1. Should capital rules be tilted for climate?\n• Against: Carney (p. 12).\n• Mixed: Bolton et al. set out both a green supporting factor and a brown penalising factor under Pillar 1 and say discussions are ”evolving towards favouring a 'brown penalising factor'”, because it is not obvious why green exposure would lower nonclimate risk (p. 52). They also list Pillar 2 and Pillar 3 options (pp. 52 to 53).\n• Modelled as a lever: Monasterolo (pp. 305 to 306, via Dunz et al.).\n• Why it matters for you: your dependent variable is capital adequacy, so this debate is your policy-relevance paragraph.\n2. Is climate risk priced?\n• Yes, in several markets: Giglio et al. (pp. 25 to 32).\n• Not yet, or mixed: Monasterolo (p. 309); Zhou et al. report heterogeneity by income, regulation and capital (p. 249).\n3. Can history-based models measure it?\n• No: Bolton et al. (p. 21), Monasterolo (pp. 300, 307 to 308), Carney (p. 8). Zhou et al. add that statistical estimates are local, linear and short-term (p. 248).\n4. What is the right discount rate and carbon price?\n• Giglio et al. (pp. 19 to 21) show the sign of the climate risk premium depends on the shock type. Carney's carbon price corridor (p. 15) is the policy translation.\n\nHow Layer 6 connects to the other layers\n• Layer 2 (Stern, Nordhaus, Pindyck, Weitzman): Giglio et al. Section 2 shows why they disagree; Monasterolo p. 307 repeats the point for risk modelling.\n• Layer 3 (Basel, stress testing, RBI): Bolton et al. Chapters 3 and 4 (the Dutch stress test, p. 36; Pillars, pp. 50 to 53) and Carney pp. 15 to 16 give the climate-specific case; Monasterolo Section 6 gives the network version."},{"pdf":187,"printed":172,"ch":7,"text":"• Layer 4 (spirals and contagion): Carney p. 11 (pro-cyclical crystallisation of losses), Bolton's climate Minsky moment (p. 7), Monasterolo's DebtRank stress test (p. 314).\n• Layer 5 (asset pricing, event studies, survey): Giglio et al. pp. 19 to 21 and 28 use the SDF logic; Zhou et al. p. 242 and Giglio et al. p. 26 use event studies; the Stroebel-Wurgler survey is the practitioner counterpart.\n\nWhere your thesis sits (the gap Layer 6 leaves)\nNone of the five sources has bank-capital evidence for an emerging market, India, or public sector banks, and none of them estimates how transition risk moves a capital adequacy ratio. Giglio et al. (p. 32) and Zhou et al. (p. 247) each name part of that gap. That is your contribution statement.\n\nWhat was and was not read\nTable 7.4. Master quotables and how Layer 6 feeds the thesis: What was and was not read Source\n\nRead\n\nNot read\n\nBolton et al.\n\nExecutive summary, introduction, most of\nChapter 2 (skipping 2.2), Chapter 3 except\nthe middle of 3.1/Box 3, 4.1, 4.2, 4.4,\nconclusion\n\nForewords, 4.3, 4.5, Annexes 1 to 4\n\nCarney\n\nWhole speech\n\nNothing\n\nGiglio, Kelly and\nStroebel\n\nMain text, pp. 15 to 32\n\nReference list\n\nMonasterolo\n\nMain text, pp. 299 to 316\n\nReference list; table figures are\ngarbled in extraction\n\nZhou, Endendijk\nand Botzen\n\nMain text, pp. 233 to 250\n\nReference list; supplementary\ntables A1 to A5\n\nBecause the three journal pieces are reviews, any number they report from another study should be cited as reported in the review, or checked at the original."},{"pdf":188,"printed":173,"ch":7,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 6"},{"pdf":189,"printed":174,"ch":7,"text":""},{"pdf":190,"printed":175,"ch":7,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 6"},{"pdf":191,"printed":176,"ch":7,"text":""}],"cards":[]};